WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.000
This is Hidden Brain, I'm Shankar Vedanta. All of us have days when despite our best efforts, everything just goes wrong. Sometimes terribly wrong. It was a devastating problem. Atul Gawande is a surgeon at Brighamon Women's Hospital in Boston. He's also a writer. You might have read his work in the New Yorker or in Slate. A few years ago, Atul was at work, operating on a patient he refers to as Mr. Hagueman.
这里是 Hidden Brain，我是尚卡尔·韦丹塔。我们所有人都有过这样的日子：尽管我们付出了最大的努力，但所有事情还是都出了岔子。有时甚至是糟透了。这是一个令人痛心的难题。阿图尔·加万德是波士顿布莱根妇女医院的一名外科医生。他同时也是一名作家。你可能在《纽约客》或《Slate》杂志上读过他的文章。几年前，阿图尔在工作中为一位他称之为哈古曼先生的病人做手术。

2
00:00:29.000 --> 00:01:20.000
I was taking out a tumor of the adrenal gland laparoscopically. He'd performed this procedure dozens of times before, but this time was particularly tricky. Mr. Hagueman's tumor was behind his liver nestled tightly against an important blood vessel. The main blood vessel returning the blood of the body to the heart. It's known as the Vena Keva. Atul began the surgery and everything was going smoothly. He was almost done detaching the tumor when all of a sudden he nicked the blood vessel. I ended up creating a hole in the Vena Kava, which meant that he then pretty quickly lost his entire blood volume into his abdomen, complete blackout on the screen and utter chaos.
我正在用腹腔镜切除一处肾上腺肿瘤。他以前做过几十次这种手术，但这次格外棘手。哈古曼先生的肿瘤长在肝脏后面，紧紧贴着一条重要的血管。那是将全身血液输送回心脏的主血管。它被称为下腔静脉。阿图尔开始做手术，一切都进展顺利。他快要把肿瘤剥离下来时，突然间，他刮破了那条血管。我最终在下腔静脉上弄出了一个洞，这意味着他的全身血液随后很快就流光到了腹腔里，监视器屏幕上一片漆黑，场面极其混乱。

3
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:46.000
Atul took the man's heart in his hand and began compressing it to keep blood flowing to his brain. I mean, he lost basically 10 times his body volume in blood, but we were able to give him enough blood to keep his circulation going. He had a cardiac arrest twice. We were finally able to repair the hole in the Vena Cave and get the tumor out and have him recover. A happy ending and a first blush,
阿图尔用手托住这个男人的心脏，开始挤压它，以保持血液流向他的大脑。我的意思是，他流失的血量基本上达到了他体重的10倍，但我们得以输给他足够的血液来维持他的血液循环。他心脏骤停了两次。我们终于修补好了下腔静脉上的洞，切除了肿瘤，并让他恢复了健康。初听之下，这是一个大团圆结局，

4
00:01:46.000 --> 00:02:16.000
a textbook case of medical heroics. A doctor makes a mistake, but he fixes it, taking a heart into his bare hands and squeezing life back into his patient's body. This is the stuff of countless movies and TV shows. Our whole idea of the hero is the person who can kind of know it all, save the day on their own just by force of their brain and their skill. But skill and brain power were not the reason Mr. Hagueman survived.
一个堪称教科书级别的医疗英雄主义案例。医生犯了一个错误，但他弥补了过失，赤手空拳托起一颗心脏，将生命重新挤回病人的体内。这是无数电影和电视剧的常见桥段。我们对英雄的全部概念，就是那种似乎无所不知，仅凭一己之力以及自身的头脑和技能就能挽救危局的人。但技能和头脑并不是哈古曼先生能够幸存的原因。

5
00:02:16.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Atul says what actually saved his patient's life was a plan the surgical team had made before they began the surgery. The plan wasn't grand or complicated. In fact, it was a humble checklist. And what happened was, when we ran the checklist, when we got to the part where we said, what's the goal of the operation and tell me anything unexpected about this. And I mentioned to the anesthesiologist that this tumor was pretty tightly against the Vena Kava. The anesthesiologist then made a plan to get more blood into the room just in case. Just in case. When disaster came he was prepared and that was the only reason that this man got through it.
阿图尔说，真正救了他病人一命的，是外科团队在手术开始前制定的一项计划。这个计划并不宏大，也不复杂。事实上，它只是一份简陋的检查清单。事情的经过是这样的：当我们过一遍检查清单时，当我们进行到讨论手术目标以及询问本次手术是否有任何意料之外的情况这一部分时，我向麻醉师提到，这个肿瘤非常紧贴着下腔静脉。于是麻醉师制定了一个计划，在手术室里多准备一些血，以防万一。以防万一。当灾难降临时，他做好了准备，而这正是这个人能够挺过难关的唯一原因。

